1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,652
AI Agentic Enterprises.

2
00:00:01,652 --> 00:00:07,039
Founder Yuvaraj Aravindan has been solving enterprise IT for three decades.

3
00:00:07,039 --> 00:00:13,450
Today: where should an AI agent run the code it writes, when the data is regulated?

4
00:00:13,450 --> 00:00:20,330
AI agents now write and run their own code, against claims, payments and patient records.

5
00:00:20,330 --> 00:00:26,039
If that code can reach the host, the network, or leaves no evidence, no regulator will sign

6
00:00:26,039 --> 00:00:26,740
off.

7
00:00:26,740 --> 00:00:32,500
So we built one governed agent in Java, with Spring AI and LangGraph4j.

8
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:34,775
The model sees only the schema.

9
00:00:34,775 --> 00:00:41,599
It writes Python, the code runs in a throwaway sandbox, errors are retried, and every step is

10
00:00:41,599 --> 00:00:44,030
hashed into an audit trail.

11
00:00:44,030 --> 00:00:44,840
Option one.

12
00:00:44,840 --> 00:00:50,270
Kata Containers on Kubernetes, managed by the Agent Sandbox controller.

13
00:00:50,270 --> 00:00:52,124
The host runs kernel 7.

14
00:00:52,124 --> 00:00:58,252
Inside the sandbox, 6.18: its own virtual machine. Network denied by policy.

15
00:00:58,252 --> 00:01:01,640
Answer in about four seconds.

16
00:01:01,640 --> 00:01:02,641
Option two.

17
00:01:02,641 --> 00:01:06,480
Google's GKE Sandbox, powered by gVisor.

18
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:12,456
The agent's service account can create sandboxes, and nothing else. It can't read secrets.

19
00:01:12,456 --> 00:01:17,490
Inside, gVisor answers as its own kernel. Under six seconds.

20
00:01:17,490 --> 00:01:18,505
Option three.

21
00:01:18,505 --> 00:01:22,530
NVIDIA OpenShell, switched to its microVM driver.

22
00:01:22,530 --> 00:01:30,340
Again, its own kernel, and the outbound internet call is refused. About seven seconds.

23
00:01:30,340 --> 00:01:34,500
The infrastructure: one Google Cloud project.

24
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:41,268
Two N2 virtual machines with nested virtualization, so no bare metal, and one GKE cluster with

25
00:01:41,268 --> 00:01:45,750
a gVisor node pool. Everything scales to zero when idle.

26
00:01:45,750 --> 00:01:51,190
Three sandboxes, one question, and the same verified answer every time.

27
00:01:51,190 --> 00:01:57,360
BCBS has the highest denial rate, with missing prior authorization the top reason.

28
00:01:57,360 --> 00:02:03,960
Kubernetes alone doesn't isolate untrusted code. Kata, gVisor or a microVM does.

29
00:02:03,960 --> 00:02:05,911
Configuration decides safety.

30
00:02:05,911 --> 00:02:11,810
And keep the agent fixed: choose the isolation each client's risk appetite needs.

31
00:02:11,810 --> 00:02:19,340
Want governed AI agents built for your regulated enterprise? Visit ai-agentic-enterprises.com.

